---
// the react implementation: what the subsite holds, and the setup. the setup
// itself is one file, stories/react/setup.md, which the react subsite's
// "getting started" renders too.
import Doc from '../../layouts/Doc.astro';
import { Content as Setup, getHeadings } from '../../../../stories/react/setup.md';
import { url, REACT, reactDocs } from '../../data/site';

const headings = [
  { depth: 2, slug: 'the-react-subsite', text: 'the react subsite' },
  ...getHeadings(),
  { depth: 2, slug: 'typescript', text: 'typescript' },
  { depth: 2, slug: 'in-a-portal-shell', text: 'in a portal shell' },
];
---
<Doc
  frontmatter={{
    title: 'react',
    description: 'The React implementation of the nalet design system: what the React subsite holds, install, peer dependencies, the CSS and the fonts.',
    react: 'react-getting-started',
  }}
  headings={headings}
>
  <h1>react</h1>
  <p>
    The components are plain React with co-located CSS, for React 18 and 19. This site is the design guidance;
    the <a href={REACT}>React subsite</a> is the implementation.
  </p>

  <h2 id="the-react-subsite">the react subsite</h2>
  <p>The React subsite is the Storybook, at <a href={REACT}>/design-system/react/</a>. It holds:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>A page per component</strong>, with a link back to its design guidance here: the playground, where every
      prop can be tried live; the examples, each with its source; the implementation notes — the native element it
      renders, the attributes it sets, its keyboard map; and the props table.
    </li>
    <li><strong>The brand glyphs</strong> — <code>Chevron</code>, <code>Lockup</code> and <code>Icon</code> — with their props.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>The patterns' code</strong>: the <code>Worklist</code> and <code>Portal</code> floorplans with their props, and the
      full examples of <a href={reactDocs('patterns-selection-and-bulk-actions')}>selection and bulk actions</a> and{' '}
      <a href={reactDocs('patterns-forms-and-settings')}>forms and settings</a>.
    </li>
    <li><strong>Getting started</strong> (the setup below) and the changelog.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The examples on this site and on the React subsite are the same stories, written once in{' '}
    <code>stories/**/*.stories.tsx</code>: the React subsite shows them with their code, this site renders them through
    Storybook's portable stories.
  </p>

  <Setup />

  <h2 id="typescript">typescript</h2>
  <p>
    The package carries its type declarations. Every component exports its props type next to it (<code>ButtonProps</code>,{' '}
    <code>DataTableColumn</code>, <code>ObjectState</code> …), and <code>defineStates</code> checks a status table against the
    keys you list. The typed tokens are on <a href={url('develop/tokens/')}>tokens</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="in-a-portal-shell">in a portal shell</h2>
  <p>
    An app that runs as a federated module inside the portal shell takes the design system from the shell, so a page
    never carries two versions of it. See <a href={url('develop/apps-in-a-shell/')}>apps in a shell</a>.
  </p>
</Doc>
